Breadth First Search (BFS)
visits the nodes in increasing order of their distance from the starting node. -> we can calculate the distance from the starting node to all other nodes using BFS.
BFS is more difficult to implement than DFS.

*Distance: the minimum number of edges from starting node to particular node. - Shortest path length in unweighted graph.

First the search explores the nodes whose distance from starting node is 1, then 2, and so on.

Time complexity O(n + m).

queue<int> q;
bool visited[N];
int dist[N];

q contains nodes to be processed in increasing order of their dist. New nodes are always added to the end of the queue, and the node at beginning of the queue is the next to be processed. The array visited indicates which nodes the search has already visited. the array dist will contain the dists from starting node to all nodes
of the graph.

void bfs(int x) {
    visited[x] = true;
    dist[x] = 0;
    q.push(x);
    while (!q.empty()) {
        int s = q.front(); q.pop();
        // process node s
        for (auto u : adj[s]) {
            if (visited[u]) continue;
            visited[u] = true;
            dist[u] = dist[s]+1;
            q.push(u);
        }
    }
}

Application:
Traversal Algorithm to check properties of Graph.
In practice, DFS is better choice, because it is easier to implement.

*Connectivity:
A Graph is connected if there is always a path between any two nodes. We can check by starting at
arbitrary node and finding out if we can reach all other nodes.
We can also find all connected components of a graph by iterating through the nodes and always
starting a new dfs if the current node does not belong to any component yet.

*Finding Cycles:
A graph contains a cycle if during a graph traversal, we find a node whose neighbor  (other than the previous node in the current path) has already been visited.

** "previous node" means parent node of the "neighbor".

Another way to check is to simply calculate the number of nodes and edges in every component. If a component contains c nodes and no cycle, it must contain exactly c-1 edges (it has to be a tree).

* Bipartiteness check

A graph is bipartite if its nodes can be colored using 2 colors so that there are no adjacent nodes with the same
color.

The idea is to color the starting node blue, all its neighbor red, all their neighbor blue, and so on. If there are 2 adjacent nodes have the same color, this means the graph is not bipartite.
